{"name":"Melancholia","hex":"#12390d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"melancholia","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Deep sadness or gloom; melancholy","depression, characterised by irrational fears, guilt and apathy"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/melancholia"}],"entries":[{"title":"Melancholia","blocks":[{"text":"Melancholia or melancholy is a concept found throughout ancient, medieval, and premodern medicine in Europe that describes a condition characterized by markedly depressed mood, bodily complaints, and sometimes hallucinations and delusions. Besides a pathological condition, melancholy could also refer to a mood or temperament and at times it was even used as a description of the human condition in general."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/A_man_whose_face_exemplifies_the_melancholy_temperament._Dra_Wellcome_V0009108ER.jpg/330px-A_man_whose_face_exemplifies_the_melancholy_temperament._Dra_Wellcome_V0009108ER.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melancholia","wikidata":"Q192077"},{"title":"Melancholia (2011 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Melancholia is a 2011 science fiction drama film written and directed by Lars von Trier and starring Kirsten Dunst, Charlotte Gainsbourg, and Kiefer Sutherland, with Alexander Skarsgård, Brady Corbet, Cameron Spurr, Charlotte Rampling, Jesper Christensen, John Hurt, Stellan Skarsgård, and Udo Kier in supporting roles. The film's story revolves around two sisters, one of whom marries just before a rogue planet is about to collide with Earth. Melancholia is the second film in von Trier's unofficially titled Depression Trilogy. It was preceded in 2009 by Antichrist and followed by Nymphomaniac in 2013."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/71/Melancholia_%282011_film_poster%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melancholia_(2011_film)","wikidata":"Q165699"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer_-_Melencolia_I_-_Google_Art_Project_%28_AGDdr3EHmNGyA%29.jpg/500px-Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer_-_Melencolia_I_-_Google_Art_Project_%28_AGDdr3EHmNGyA%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer_-_Melencolia_I_-_Google_Art_Project_%28_AGDdr3EHmNGyA%29.jpg/1280px-Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer_-_Melencolia_I_-_Google_Art_Project_%28_AGDdr3EHmNGyA%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer_-_Melencolia_I_-_Google_Art_Project_%28_AGDdr3EHmNGyA%29.jpg/500px-Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer_-_Melencolia_I_-_Google_Art_Project_%28_AGDdr3EHmNGyA%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Melencolia I by Albrecht Dürer, 1514","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlbrecht_D%C3%BCrer_-_Melencolia_I_-_Google_Art_Project_(_AGDdr3EHmNGyA).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Robert_Burton%27s_Anatomy_of_Melancholy%2C_1626%2C_2nd_edition.jpg/500px-Robert_Burton%27s_Anatomy_of_Melancholy%2C_1626%2C_2nd_edition.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Robert_Burton%27s_Anatomy_of_Melancholy%2C_1626%2C_2nd_edition.jpg/1280px-Robert_Burton%27s_Anatomy_of_Melancholy%2C_1626%2C_2nd_edition.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Robert_Burton%27s_Anatomy_of_Melancholy%2C_1626%2C_2nd_edition.jpg/500px-Robert_Burton%27s_Anatomy_of_Melancholy%2C_1626%2C_2nd_edition.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=t